# Machine-local hosts first — work jump boxes, per-host keys, anything whose
# name would leak in a public repo. Untracked and absent from this repo; ssh
# ignores a missing include silently, so a fresh machine needs no extra setup.
#
# Its position is load-bearing, and is the opposite of what ~/.gitconfig does
# with the same trick. Git takes the last value it reads; ssh takes the first,
# and never revisits a keyword once something has set it. So this has to come
# before the Host * block below, or the defaults there would be the first
# match for every keyword and the local file could never override any of them.
Include ~/.ssh/config.local

# Where the control sockets go. Above the Host * block for the same first-match
# reason as the include: a ControlPath set there would be the first value read,
# and neither branch below could replace it.
#
# On the distros the socket belongs under /run/user. It is runtime state that
# must not outlive the boot, and that is a tmpfs logind creates per user and
# clears on logout — `loginctl enable-linger` holds it open past that, but it is
# tmpfs either way, so nothing survives a reboot. macOS has no /run at all, and
# an unusable ControlPath is not a soft failure there: ssh authenticates, then
# dies with "unix_listener: cannot bind to path", exit 255, on every single
# connection. Hence a probe rather than one hardcoded path — and one that tests
# for a writable directory rather than merely a present one, since a /run/user
# that exists but is not ours lands straight back on that same error.
#
# The fallback is ~/.ssh rather than anywhere under /tmp, which is world-
# writable and outlives a logout. Its cost is smaller than it looks: a master
# killed uncleanly — crash, kill -9, suspend — leaves a dead socket file, but
# the next connection to that destination finds it refused, logs "Stale control
# socket, unlinking" at debug1 and takes over as master. Nothing to remove by
# hand, and nothing printed at the default log level. The case that does need a
# hand is a non-socket sitting at the path — a restored backup, a sync client —
# which ssh will not unlink, and which silently costs multiplexing.
#
# %C in both branches. sun_path caps a socket path at 104 bytes here, 108 on
# Linux, and ssh binds a longer temporary name before linking it into place, so
# the usable budget is smaller again. %C spends a fixed 40 characters on a
# digest of the whole destination — local and remote host, port, user, any jump
# host — so no hostname however long can overrun it. That the local host is in
# there also means the socket name changes when this machine is renamed, and a
# persisting master is not reused across it.
Match exec "test -d /run/user/%i && test -w /run/user/%i"
	ControlPath /run/user/%i/ssh-%C
Match all
	ControlPath ~/.ssh/cm-%C

Host *
	# Load a key into the agent the first time it is used, rather than only
	# ever when ssh-add is run by hand — one passphrase prompt per login
	# instead of one per connection.
	AddKeysToAgent yes

	# Without these a connection that dies quietly — a NAT table dropping an
	# idle mapping, a laptop changing network — leaves a session that looks
	# alive and hangs forever on the next keystroke. Three missed probes at
	# 60s ends it instead.
	ServerAliveInterval 60
	ServerAliveCountMax 3

	# Reuse one authenticated connection for every later session to the same
	# host, so they skip the TCP handshake, key exchange and auth entirely.
	# Most visible where a command opens its own connection each time it runs:
	# a fetch-then-push cycle over ssh pays that setup twice otherwise.
	ControlMaster auto
	ControlPersist 10m
